GDP measures production; real growth adjusts prices; productivity concerns output per input; cycles describe fluctuations.|Nominal output rises 8% while prices rise 5%.|Real growth is necessarily 8%?|No|Yes|Only with unchanged employment
Inflation raises the general price level; disinflation slows its increase; deflation lowers it.|Inflation falls from 8% to 4% while prices still rise.|This is…?|Disinflation|Deflation|Zero inflation
Employment and wages affect purchasing power and demand; consumption also depends on expectations and credit.|Nominal wages rise 3% while prices rise 6%.|Real wage purchasing power generally…?|Falls|Rises|Stays unchanged
Money aggregates, bank credit, and market liquidity are related but distinct concepts.|Credit expands while an individual bond remains hard to sell.|More aggregate money guarantees every asset is liquid?|No|Yes|Only for bonds
Monetary transmission links policy to funding costs, credit, spending, exchange rates, and expectations.|Higher policy rates raise some borrowing costs with lags.|Every company's cash flow changes identically and immediately?|No|Yes|Only domestically
Policy rates price central-bank facilities; reserves affect liquidity needs; open-market operations buy or sell securities.|A central bank purchases securities, changing reserve supply.|All tools have identical mechanics?|No|Yes|Only during recessions
QE expands central-bank securities holdings; QT reduces them under specified operations, affecting liquidity and term pricing.|Bond purchases can affect yields without fixing every borrowing rate.|QE guarantees proportionate consumer-price inflation?|No|Yes|Only if large
Fiscal policy uses spending and taxes to influence demand, distribution, and public services.|Government increases infrastructure spending while financing it with borrowing.|Assess only immediate GDP effects?|No|Yes|Only during downturns
Fiscal deficits are period funding shortfalls; public debt is an accumulated stock influenced by deficits and other adjustments.|Debt ₹100 plus ₹10 deficit gives simplified ₹110 debt before other effects.|Deficit and debt are identical measures?|No|Yes|Only annually
Debt sustainability depends on growth, interest, primary balances, currency, maturity, and financing access.|Interest exceeds nominal growth while primary deficits persist.|This can pressure…?|Debt-to-GDP dynamics|Only government logos|Only bond coupons
Balance of payments records cross-border transactions; current and financial accounts reflect different flows with accounting conventions.|An import payment and foreign investment inflow occupy different categories.|Capital inflows necessarily indicate strong export competitiveness?|No|Yes|Only for emerging markets
Exchange regimes range from pegs to floating systems, with different policy constraints and intervention practices.|A peg requires credible support under pressure.|A declared peg eliminates devaluation risk?|No|Yes|Only with reserves
Sovereign risk concerns willingness and ability to honour obligations; currency and institutions matter.|Foreign-currency debt becomes harder to service after domestic depreciation.|Printing domestic currency resolves every foreign-currency obligation automatically?|No|Yes|Only for sovereigns
Financial repression directs or constrains financial returns; capital controls restrict specified cross-border flows.|Investors face limits transferring funds abroad.|Legal restrictions can affect liquidity and valuation?|Yes|No|Only for banks
Leading indicators anticipate activity imperfectly; macro forecasts require revisions and uncertainty.|Survey orders weaken before reported production falls.|One indicator guarantees the next recession date?|No|Yes|Only if historically correlated
Macro shifts affect demand, input costs, funding, cash flows, and discount rates through different channels.|Higher rates reduce some asset values while helping some deposit margins.|One macro shock affects all sectors identically?|No|Yes|Only within one country
